+---
+id: dl-005-createpatch-minimal-diff-semantics
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch minimal diff semantics"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two document versions, as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902). A patch that replaces the whole document is correct but defeats the purpose, so the patch must be proportional to the change.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `createPatch` walks both documents together. Objects are compared member by member (removals first, then adds and recursion into shared members, in key order); equal values emit nothing; differing scalars or values of different types emit a `replace` at their own path, the root included (path `""`).
+- Arrays are compared with their equal head and tail skipped. In the middle, elements at shared positions are diffed recursively, then surplus source elements are removed from the highest index down, or surplus target elements are added in ascending order. Every index is thus valid when its operation runs.
+- Only `add`, `remove` and `replace` are produced. `move`, `copy` and `test` are not: they would need a costlier search for little gain and `test` is a precondition, not a change.
+- Values are copied with `structuredClone`, so the patch shares nothing with the inputs, and inputs are never mutated. Pointer tokens are escaped (`~0`, `~1`); own-property checks keep `__proto__` plain data. Output order is deterministic.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The patch is minimal for object changes and for insertions, removals and edits at one place in an array, but not guaranteed minimal in general: a shifted or reordered array middle is expressed as replacements plus adds/removes rather than an optimal edit script or `move`. Round-trip correctness is covered by tests.
